        <div id="cfm" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. CFM at 90 PSI: The Number That Decides Everything
          </h2>

          <p>
            The best air compressor is the one with enough airflow for the tools you will run. Tank size and horsepower get the headlines, but CFM at 90 PSI is the spec that decides whether a tool works properly.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">CFM vs PSI</h3>

          <p>
            PSI is pressure: how hard the air pushes. CFM is volume: how much air flows each minute. Most air tools run at 90 PSI, so compare compressors by their CFM at 90 PSI. On this list that ranges from 2.2 CFM for the California Air Tools 8010A to 5.5 CFM for the Campbell Hausfeld VT6290.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Match the Compressor to the Tool</h3>

          <p>
            Nailers and staplers use short bursts of air, so a pancake with 2 to 3 CFM is plenty. Impact wrenches, ratchets, sanders and spray guns use air continuously and need far more. Check the CFM rating on your air tool and choose a compressor that exceeds it.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Duty Cycle</h3>

          <p>
            Duty cycle is how much of the time a compressor can run without overheating. The Campbell Hausfeld lists 80/20. Small oil-free compressors are designed for intermittent use, which suits nailing but not continuous air tools.
          </p>
        </div>

