        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            15. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best air compressor?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most home garages, the California Air Tools 8010A is the best air compressor. It is listed at 60 decibels with an 8-gallon tank and 2.2 CFM at 90 PSI, enough for nailers and inflating. The Metabo HPT EC914S is best for framing nailers, and the Campbell Hausfeld VT6290 is best for air tools, with 5.5 CFM at 90 PSI.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best portable air compressor?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For shop work, a pancake like the CRAFTSMAN 6-gallon kit or the Metabo HPT EC914S is the most portable full-size option. For car tires, a portable inflator like the DEWALT DCC020IB is more convenient: it runs from a 20V battery, a 12V car outlet or household power, and shuts off automatically at the set pressure.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What size air compressor do I need for a home garage?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For nailers, staplers, inflating and blowing dust, a 6- to 8-gallon compressor with 2 to 3 CFM at 90 PSI is enough. For framing nailers, look for about 4 CFM. For impact wrenches, sanders and spray guns, choose 20 gallons or more with at least 5 CFM at 90 PSI.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best air compressor for car tires?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A portable inflator like the DEWALT 20V MAX DCC020IB is the easiest, with automatic shut-off and three power sources. Any shop compressor with a tire chuck also works well. Inflate to the pressure on the driver's door jamb sticker, checked with an accurate gauge.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are oil-free air compressors good?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, for most homeowners. Oil-free compressors need no oil changes and are lighter; the California Air Tools pump is rated for over 3,000 hours. Oil-lubricated pumps run cooler and last longer, which matters for heavy, regular air tool use, but they need oil checks and changes.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the quietest air compressor?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The Metabo HPT EC28M is listed at 59 dB and the California Air Tools 8010A at 60 dBA, about the level of normal conversation. By comparison, the Makita MAC2400 lists 79 dB and the Campbell Hausfeld VT6290 82 to 85 dBA. Slow pump speeds are what make quiet compressors quiet.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use an extension cord with an air compressor?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It is better not to. Long or thin extension cords reduce voltage to the motor, which can trip breakers and damage it. Plug the compressor directly into a dedicated outlet and run a longer air hose to where you are working instead.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

